{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1616 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "0a91cbb87f91833b0be21d79ef41f172388c19d7fdd6770ad0f87b117ccf30f6" :edb/db-before-t 1615 :edb/db-after-t 1616 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534928 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:15:42.958Z" 13194139534928 true] [17592186184005 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186184005 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" 13194139534928 false] [17592186184006 1100 "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186184006 1100 "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" 13194139534928 false] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320454 13194139534928 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320461 13194139534928 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320475 13194139534928 true] [17592186184094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320480 13194139534928 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320447 13194139534928 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320490 13194139534928 true] [17592186184095 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320497 13194139534928 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320468 13194139534928 true] [17592186184096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320485 13194139534928 true] [17592186184546 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534928 true] [17592186184546 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x) = 2x - \\ln (1 + x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 false] [17592186184546 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 true] [17592186184546 1145 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{1+x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne -1$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is concave up on its entire domain, $(-1,\\infty ).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186184685 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 true] [17592186184685 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 false] [17592186293687 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534928 true] [17592186293687 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534928 false] [17592186303129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 true] [17592186303129 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 false] [17592186303136 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139534928 true] [17592186303136 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139534928 false] [17592186320447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320448 13194139534928 true] [17592186320447 1089 "q-114272" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534928 true] [17592186320447 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320447 1113 #uuid "067c622d-d840-5844-b7eb-4a94820f230e" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320447 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320451 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320452 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1041 "selection" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320448 1110 #uuid "fe6da3f2-ab9e-5fc4-b2f0-3c180a33303a" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320449 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320449 1111 #uuid "560f6e49-18ff-5174-980c-720bcf8ef38f" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320450 1034 "I only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320450 1111 #uuid "22c091c6-f7b5-5527-b8b1-c873c7d06dd0" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320451 1034 "III only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320451 1111 #uuid "957940e7-7f35-58a3-a0e4-daa55621d3c6" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320452 1034 "None of the statements are true." 13194139534928 true] [17592186320452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320452 1111 #uuid "68f85aa4-9ff4-5d41-b9a6-047f340b4436" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320453 1034 "II only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320453 1111 #uuid "d781e2b1-7b73-53f5-aa5e-9598d05ee3c2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320455 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1089 "q-2458" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11$ is concave down." 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1113 #uuid "61f4c5d5-e624-5f18-a1ff-0dcd8d62b8ff" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320454 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-6x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+4 & <0 \\\\ 6x & >4 \\\\ x & >\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320456 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320457 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320458 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320459 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320460 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1041 "selection" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320459 13194139534928 true] [17592186320455 1110 #uuid "900a2d18-6343-53b5-bfe4-98b15da