rkers/multivariable/14043400/assets/5287302ea411868ac0d2f8d06e5487dabcb421f6e462e0af0db30e34c328b8d4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={g}^{″}(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up." 13194139534928 true] [17592186320468 1113 #uuid "b3c86bc2-98d4-5fb5-acf1-a49b3c614ac3" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320468 1145 "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,5)$ only. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,5)$ only." 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320470 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320471 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320472 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320473 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320474 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1041 "selection" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320472 13194139534928 true] [17592186320469 1110 #uuid "bbba0dd4-3ab9-5461-9f7b-85df9f9b0720" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320470 1034 "(1,5)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320470 1111 #uuid "ad264db1-5529-538f-8016-ec81fa14e725" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320471 1034 "(-1,1)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320471 1111 #uuid "e9b37a24-5046-5f7e-a66e-088190e134db" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320472 1034 "(0,5)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320472 1111 #uuid "ca3deccf-a9d7-5fad-9be3-8b1b84f41106" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320473 1034 "(-5,5)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320473 1111 #uuid "46016a84-8d64-5e42-b17f-5926e610ce6a" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320474 1034 "(-5,0)" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320474 1111 #uuid "c281e42a-ffbd-5a2f-8740-11952dc63d89" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320476 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320478 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1089 "q-344331" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1100 "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1113 #uuid "4d764786-e782-55ee-95fb-818969f26457" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320475 1145 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=7x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =7-\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne 2$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is $(\\,2,\\infty \\,).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320477 13194139534928 true] [17592186320476 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320476 1041 "field-1" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320476 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320477 13194139534928 true] [17592186320476 1110 #uuid "628f8670-995e-536c-a534-9a03e1d5940a" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320477 1034 "2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320477 1111 #uuid "0d7edf00-96b3-5ec0-af86-cd6468e0759e" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320478 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320479 13194139534928 true] [17592186320478 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320478 1041 "field-2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320478 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320479 13194139534928 true] [17592186320478 1110 #uuid "31cf0912-1e14-556e-a8cc-3fe0e293dd2d" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320479 1034 "\\infty" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320479 1111 #uuid "ba1f896c-e81a-5be4-b7d3-49657a24dec4" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320481 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320483 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1089 "q-344352" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1100 "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1113 #uuid "936f4aaf-184a-570f-bb32-26f39de342aa" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320480 1145 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -4.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3(x+4)-(3x+1)}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{(x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =11(x+4)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-22(x+4)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-22(x+4)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+4)^{-3} & <0 \\\\ x+4 & <0 \\\\ x & <-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320481 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320482 13194139534928 true] [17592186320481 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320481 1041 "field-1" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320481 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320482 13194139534928 true] [17592186320481 1110 #uuid "f82ef3b2-4fe9-56d1-92d3-e70dcff0fbbb" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320482 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320482 1111 #uuid "480293e6-fc6c-54b6-896a-579231a80508" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320484 13194139534928 true] [17592186320483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320483 1041 "field-2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320484 13194139534928 true] [17592186320483 1110 #uuid "28224180-a635-5de2-9040-4d515801a86c" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320484 1034 "-4" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320484 1111 #uuid "5b3954b9-d708-5d91-8adc-dfa35c920151" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320486 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320488 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1089 "q-344777" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/f03d1f23687273ff94bb2ea4ee45991600c75a237bf4c0a1fdb955d0f00b9b3d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-2$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n$f(x)$  is concave down on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1113 #uuid "2582ee8e-344a-5728-9b4e-ca914cf6725d" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320485 1145 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can  see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval   $(-2,3)$ \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(\\,-2,3\\,).$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320486 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320487 13194139534928 true] [17592186320486 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320486 1041 "field-1" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320486 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320487 13194139534928 true] [17592186320486 1110 #uuid "b9e8ce41-45ed-5eeb-b844-37dbc565c0cc" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320487 1034 "-2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320487 1111 #uuid "1c4e2c60-9165-5de2-a999-849a8f927e13" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320488 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320489 13194139534928 true] [17592186320488 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534928 true] [17592186320488 1041 "field-2" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320488 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320489 13194139534928 true] [17592186320488 1110 #uuid "997050a4-b25f-5b38-b8db-23b3a0ce17cb" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320489 1034 "3" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534928 true] [17592186320489 1111 #uuid "8f1ffeb5-af63-59db-b1c7-767c916cdeff" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320491 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1089 "q-79039" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1113 #uuid "e13c31ca-a0e6-5bea-ba02-5659bc22c857" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320490 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,⟹\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320492 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320493 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320494 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320495 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320496 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1041 "selection" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320496 13194139534928 true] [17592186320491 1110 #uuid "817c1966-13a6-50c5-ade6-a5fa4e9b965a" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320492 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320492 1111 #uuid "071199a4-c541-520b-a754-ef475c1124d3" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320493 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320493 1111 #uuid "2ebbe0c5-c58e-57ee-bd17-a9a6110131d9" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320494 1034 "I only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320494 1111 #uuid "1fe041b9-ed27-5f4b-b0f5-d41c8c4dcafc" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320495 1034 "III only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320495 1111 #uuid "287708a4-6902-5218-b5cf-4db66cd59ab5" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320496 1034 "II only" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534928 true] [17592186320496 1111 #uuid "a9fe613d-89a4-55e6-aab9-5fc2bcd7e307" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320498 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1089 "q-79040" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-2x$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,2)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1113 #uuid "5f04afe1-6ba3-5a8c-bdc7-0d8962a4537a" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320497 1145 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x=12x(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x(x-2) & >0 \\\\ x(x-2) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/assets/cb06747b13b36bc1b53f96df8d1345332200ecb8b7728342d7086963b51d86f5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-2)>0$ when $x<0$ and $x>2.$ So the graph is concave up on the intervals  $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-2)<0,$ which has the solution $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534928 true] [17592186320498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320499 13194139534928 true] [17592186320498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320500 13194139534928 true] [17592186320498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320501 13194139534928 true] [17592186320498 1000 #e